WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.470
Instructor: Okay, in this video,

00:01.470 --> 00:04.920
I wanna talk about deleting and renaming branches.

00:04.920 --> 00:06.810
So, this is something you probably won't need to do

00:06.810 --> 00:08.940
all that often, early on at least.

00:08.940 --> 00:11.070
But if you work professionally

00:11.070 --> 00:12.450
or you work on a large project,

00:12.450 --> 00:13.865
where there's tons of branches

00:13.865 --> 00:16.500
and you are constantly making new branches

00:16.500 --> 00:17.850
and then merging them in,

00:17.850 --> 00:19.620
again, we haven't talked about merging,

00:19.620 --> 00:21.420
but it's really common to work on something

00:21.420 --> 00:22.860
and then merge it in.

00:22.860 --> 00:25.440
You don't wanna just have this extra branch for no reason,

00:25.440 --> 00:27.210
so you can delete them.

00:27.210 --> 00:29.307
Let's talk about how you delete a branch.

00:29.307 --> 00:31.950
You use the git-branch command.

00:31.950 --> 00:33.570
Git-branch does a lot and we've seen,

00:33.570 --> 00:35.940
it shows us a list of branches.

00:35.940 --> 00:37.950
It also creates a new branch

00:37.950 --> 00:42.950
and we can delete using git-branch with the dash D option.

00:43.650 --> 00:47.880
So, dash D or dash dash delete deletes a branch.

00:47.880 --> 00:49.290
Now, it tells us this message here,

00:49.290 --> 00:51.180
it must be fully merged, blah blah blah.

00:51.180 --> 00:52.861
We will learn more about what this means shortly.

00:52.861 --> 00:54.821
We haven't talked about merging.

00:54.821 --> 00:57.360
But if I try and delete one of these branches.

00:57.360 --> 01:00.480
For example, let's make a new branch.

01:00.480 --> 01:02.370
So, I'll go full screen here.

01:02.370 --> 01:03.360
Where are we?

01:03.360 --> 01:04.620
We are on an empty playlist.

01:04.620 --> 01:05.670
It's an empty branch.

01:05.670 --> 01:08.910
Let's go to, I don't know, Georges branch.

01:08.910 --> 01:11.610
So, git switch Georges.

01:11.610 --> 01:13.080
And then let's make a new branch

01:13.080 --> 01:15.690
and I'll use our single line version,

01:15.690 --> 01:19.200
git switch dash C to create a new branch.

01:19.200 --> 01:21.540
And I'm gonna call it, delete me

01:21.540 --> 01:24.240
because I want to delete it.

01:24.240 --> 01:26.910
Okay, so we're on this new branch, delete me.

01:26.910 --> 01:31.500
And to delete it, I use the dash D option on git-branch.

01:31.500 --> 01:32.550
So, let's try it.

01:32.550 --> 01:37.550
Git branch dash D and then I specify the branch to delete,

01:37.980 --> 01:39.390
which is delete me.

01:39.390 --> 01:40.877
I'm gonna run into a problem.

01:40.877 --> 01:43.830
I can't delete the branch I'm checked out at.

01:43.830 --> 01:46.890
So, the one that I'm viewing, right, I'm on delete me,

01:46.890 --> 01:47.940
I can't delete it.

01:47.940 --> 01:52.440
So, if I switch branches, let's do git switch master

01:52.440 --> 01:57.150
and I try running that again, now I get a different message.

01:57.150 --> 01:59.070
It's not fully merged.

01:59.070 --> 02:00.450
If you're sure you want to delete it,

02:00.450 --> 02:02.729
run git branch dash uppercase D,

02:02.729 --> 02:07.650
which is equivalent to adding in the dash dash force option.

02:07.650 --> 02:10.260
In other words, allow deleting the branch irrespective

02:10.260 --> 02:12.360
of its merged status.

02:12.360 --> 02:14.029
So, in this case, that's fine.

02:14.029 --> 02:17.100
You wanna be confident in what you're deleting

02:17.100 --> 02:20.220
and that you actually do want to delete it because

02:20.220 --> 02:22.770
without GitHub, without any sort of backup,

02:22.770 --> 02:24.510
which we don't have at this point in time,

02:24.510 --> 02:26.070
if I had a lot of work on this branch

02:26.070 --> 02:29.070
and I actually delete it, it's gone.

02:29.070 --> 02:32.190
But for this demonstration, I'm fine deleting it.

02:32.190 --> 02:36.120
So git-branch, uppercase D, delete me.

02:36.120 --> 02:37.650
And now, it's gone.

02:37.650 --> 02:40.920
So we can see here, there is no delete me branch.

02:40.920 --> 02:43.440
All right, now another thing we might want

02:43.440 --> 02:45.600
to do is rename a branch.

02:45.600 --> 02:47.576
And this is something we definitely will be doing

02:47.576 --> 02:49.521
when we get to GitHub.

02:49.521 --> 02:52.055
We could rename our master branch to main

02:52.055 --> 02:54.630
to play well with GitHub,

02:54.630 --> 02:56.917
but also it's a common thing to do.

02:56.917 --> 02:59.070
If you're unhappy with a branch name

02:59.070 --> 03:02.460
or you just want to change it and give it a better name,

03:02.460 --> 03:05.317
the way that we do it is somewhat hard to remember.

03:05.317 --> 03:07.980
We use git-branch, the same command.

03:07.980 --> 03:10.950
However, the flag or the option that we use

03:10.950 --> 03:15.120
is dash M and that seems somewhat confusing.

03:15.120 --> 03:19.410
It should be dash R for rename or dash N for name,

03:19.410 --> 03:24.030
but it's actually dash M for move.

03:24.030 --> 03:26.520
But it's move slash rename.

03:26.520 --> 03:28.606
So, all that we do, if we want to rename a branch,

03:28.606 --> 03:33.150
is first change to the branch that we want to rename.

03:33.150 --> 03:35.880
So, this is kind of confusing because to delete a branch,

03:35.880 --> 03:39.390
we had to go anywhere else but that branch.

03:39.390 --> 03:42.090
We could not delete from the branch.

03:42.090 --> 03:44.610
But to rename, you have to be on the branch,

03:44.610 --> 03:46.200
again, it's just kind of weird,

03:46.200 --> 03:48.570
but you don't have to do this that frequently.

03:48.570 --> 03:51.210
So, what branch should I rename?

03:51.210 --> 03:53.742
How about instead of recentish music,

03:53.742 --> 03:56.910
I just wanna rename it to 2000s.

03:56.910 --> 03:58.290
So, music from the 2000s

03:58.290 --> 04:01.561
because recentish implies something somewhat pretty recent.

04:01.561 --> 04:04.200
but maybe I want anything from the 2000s.

04:04.200 --> 04:07.470
So, I'm gonna switch, git switch.

04:07.470 --> 04:09.690
And I'll do checkout this time just to mix it up.

04:09.690 --> 04:14.460
Show you both work, recentish music, I switched, okay.

04:14.460 --> 04:18.150
And now I'm gonna rename it with git branch dash M,

04:18.150 --> 04:21.693
and then my new name, which will be 2000s.

04:23.910 --> 04:27.360
And we can see already, it changed to 2000s over here.

04:27.360 --> 04:32.360
If I type git status on branch 2000s git log,

04:33.300 --> 04:37.950
we see that head is pointing to 2000s as the branch name.

04:37.950 --> 04:39.740
It's the same branch that I was on before,

04:39.740 --> 04:43.890
the same two Phoebe Bridger songs are here,

04:43.890 --> 04:48.890
but it's been renamed from recentish music to 2000s.

04:49.050 --> 04:51.750
So I'll do I guess one more commit.

04:51.750 --> 04:55.350
Here are two songs by a great group called Moderat

04:55.350 --> 04:59.880
and I will go ahead and commit dash A, dash M,

04:59.880 --> 05:04.880
just to do it in one go, add to Moderat songs.

05:05.820 --> 05:10.650
All right and so that only exists on the 2000s branch.

05:10.650 --> 05:15.150
It no longer is called recentish songs or recentish music.

05:15.150 --> 05:19.110
Okay, so that's how you can rename a branch, it's dash M.

05:19.110 --> 05:20.490
Where are you, where'd I do it?

05:20.490 --> 05:23.718
Right here, it's somewhat confusing, but it works.

05:23.718 --> 05:26.637
It stands for move and then we have dash D

05:26.637 --> 05:28.500
to delete a branch.

05:28.500 --> 05:30.660
Two operations you may not need to do all that often,

05:30.660 --> 05:33.543
certainly not right now, but good to know about.
